<h2>The prosecution and the shortcut</h2>
<p>The allegation was that the appellant married a fourteen-year-old girl in January 2024, and that she gave birth in November that year — facts which, if proved, constitute aggravated penetrative sexual assault under Section 6 of the POCSO Act and offences under the child marriage law. The Special Court convicted within weeks of the case reaching it, on the strength of a purported plea of guilt, and imposed the statutory minimum of twenty years.</p>
<p>The High Court found that the plea could not bear examination, let alone a two-decade sentence.</p>

<h2>Four defects, each fatal</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. No charge was ever framed.</strong> Sections 251-252 BNSS require a charge to be framed, read over and explained before a plea is taken. The record showed no formal charge at all — the accused admitted an &ldquo;accusation,&rdquo; not specified offences.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. The plea record was unsigned.</strong> Neither the appellant&rsquo;s nor the judge&rsquo;s signature appeared on the plea record, and a later explanation by the trial judge could not substitute for the contemporaneous record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Age was never proved.</strong> The survivor&rsquo;s age — the ingredient that makes the POCSO Act applicable at all — was established by no document and no witness. Without the foundational facts, the Section 29 presumption never arose.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Conviction without notice.</strong> The appellant was charged under Section 9 of the PCM Act but convicted under Section 10 — a different offence, without notice or opportunity to meet it.</div>
</div>

<h2>The appeal bar that was not</h2>
<p>The State&rsquo;s structural answer — that Section 416 BNSS bars appeals from convictions on a plea of guilty — received the judgment&rsquo;s most consequential holding: the bar presupposes a legally valid plea. A plea extracted without a charge, unrecorded in the manner the statute commands, and unsigned, is no plea in law; and what is no plea in law cannot shut the appellate door. The reasoning rests on the statute read with Article 21&rsquo;s fair-trial guarantee — notably, the Court needed no case law at all.</p>
<div class="note">The seriousness of the sentence drives the strictness of the procedure. Where Parliament prescribes a twenty-year minimum, a plea of guilt must be voluntary, informed and unequivocal — the accused must know precisely what he is admitting and what follows from it. The graver the consequence, the less room there is for shortcuts.</div>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<p>For defence counsel, the case is a checklist for auditing any guilty-plea conviction: the charge, its reading over and explanation, the verbatim record of the plea, the signatures, the proof of jurisdictional ingredients, and the match between the charge and the conviction. A defect at any point reopens the appeal route notwithstanding the statutory bar. For prosecutors and Special Courts, it is a reminder that pleas in minimum-sentence offences are not docket-clearing devices; a conviction obtained in weeks and reversed in a year serves neither the survivor nor the system. And for the trial now ordered, everything remains open — the remit restores the prosecution to the position of proving age, marriage and assault by evidence, with the full protections of a POCSO trial for the child witness.</p>
